e for `cubic_observe`. Looking at how BBR implements it as a method on `BbrState`, I should make `observe` a method on `CubicState` that returns a tuple of two u64 values. There could also be a wrapper function at the module level that takes a `Path` and handles the downcast, mirroring the C API's behavior.\n\nImplementing the Rust methods... I'm thinking through the unsigned integer subtraction behavior in Rust versus C—when `current_time` is less than `start_of_epoch`, the subtraction wraps to a huge `u64` value, which then converts to a large positive float. Since this helper is only called in valid contexts where the epoch has already been set, I don't need to add extra safety checks here.\n\nTo match the C semantics exactly, I'll use `wrapping_sub` to explicitly handle the unsigned arithmetic, then cast to `f64` and divide by the microsecond conversion factor. This gives me the elapsed time in seconds, which I can then subtract `k` from to get the final delta.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3973,"cache_read_input_tokens":59347,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":3973},"output_tokens":10,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"e717f4e2-96a7-4752-b6de-ede097ef17e8","uuid":"065c2b24-0d44-4745-a045-bfac36b55ecc"}
